            <div class="post-body__intro reveal">
              <p>After 15 years and 135+ projects across Delaware County and the Main Line, I've replaced windows in 1890s Victorians in Media, 1950s ranches in Springfield, and everything in between. Every project teaches me something new. This guide shares what we actually do — the styles we recommend, the materials we spec, and the job-site realities that no manufacturer's brochure ever mentions.</p>
              <p>Choosing new windows is one of those decisions that seems simple until you get into it. There are dozens of window types, hundreds of glass options, and a wide range of price points. Most online guides walk you through the same brochure copy. This one is different — it's written from the contractor side, based on what we install in real Delaware County homes and why we make those recommendations.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Window Styles — What Works in Delaware County Homes</h2>
              <p>There is no universally "best" window style. The right choice depends on your home's architecture, the room's function, your ventilation needs, and the orientation of the opening. Here is how we typically match styles to the homes we work on in this region.</p>

              <h3>Double-Hung Windows</h3>
              <p>The default choice for most Delaware County homes — and for good reason. Both sashes open, which makes them easy to clean from inside and allows flexible ventilation. They suit the colonial and craftsman homes that dominate Media, Springfield, and Swarthmore. Double-hung windows are also the easiest to replace in a like-for-like swap, since they fit standard rough openings and don't require frame modifications in most cases.</p>

              <h3>Casement Windows</h3>
              <p>Casement windows open outward on a hinge, which gives them an airtight seal when closed — useful in living rooms and studies where energy efficiency is the priority. They're also our recommendation for any opening that is hard to reach, since the crank mechanism lets you open and close them without leaning out. We see them frequently on side walls of additions and in kitchen windows above counters.</p>

              <h3>Sliding Windows</h3>
              <p>A practical choice for wide, low openings — typical in basement-level windows and modern kitchen configurations. One or both panels slide horizontally. They are lower-profile than casements and less complex mechanically, which reduces long-term maintenance.</p>

              <h3>Picture Windows</h3>
              <p>Fixed and non-operable, picture windows maximize light and framing of an outdoor view. They work well in living rooms and front-facing walls where ventilation comes from other openings. Because they don't open, they have no moving parts and typically outlast other window types with minimal maintenance.</p>

              <h3>Bay and Bow Windows</h3>
              <p>Bay windows (three panels at angles) and bow windows (four or more panels in a curve) add depth and light to a room and increase the perceived square footage of a space. They are popular in dining rooms and living areas in the larger colonial homes throughout Delaware County. The installation is more involved — we always assess the structural header above the opening before recommending this style, because bay windows add weight to the facade and sometimes require additional support.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Materials — Matching Frame Material to Your Home</h2>
              <p>The frame material you choose affects performance, maintenance, lifespan, and how the window looks from both inside and outside your home. Here is how we evaluate materials for Delaware County homes specifically.</p>

              <h3>Vinyl Frames</h3>
              <p>Our most commonly installed frame material. Vinyl does not rot, does not need painting, and holds its thermal performance over time. For most homeowners in Springfield, Broomall, and Upper Darby who want a cost-effective upgrade with minimal upkeep, vinyl is the right call. The caveat: cheap vinyl can warp in extreme temperature swings. We specify mid-to-high-grade vinyl with reinforced chambers, not entry-level contractor-grade product.</p>

              <h3>Fiberglass Frames</h3>
              <p>Fiberglass expands and contracts at nearly the same rate as glass, which means the seal holds better over time compared to vinyl. It handles Pennsylvania's hot summers and cold winters extremely well. We recommend fiberglass for second-story and exposed-elevation windows, where temperature differentials are more pronounced. The price premium over vinyl is roughly 20–35% per unit, but the long-term performance typically justifies it on those exposures.</p>

              <h3>Wood-Clad Windows</h3>
              <p>For historic homes — and Delaware County has many — wood-clad windows are often the right answer, and sometimes the required one. Homes in historic overlay districts (several neighborhoods in Media and Rose Valley) may have guidelines about window profiles and materials. Wood-clad windows with an aluminum or fiberglass exterior give you the authentic sightlines of a true wood window with far lower maintenance. We work regularly with homeowners navigating historic district approvals and can help identify what the local requirements actually are.</p>

              <h3>Aluminum Frames</h3>
              <p>Aluminum is durable and dimensionally stable, but it conducts cold easily — a real problem in Pennsylvania winters. We use it primarily in commercial work and occasionally in utility applications (garages, workshops) where thermal performance is less critical. For living spaces, we prefer vinyl or fiberglass.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Energy Efficiency — Double-Pane, Triple-Pane, and Low-E Glass</h2>
              <p>The energy efficiency conversation in Delaware County comes down to two practical questions: how exposed is the opening, and what is the home's overall envelope condition?</p>

              <h3>Double-Pane with Low-E Glass</h3>
              <p>The baseline for virtually every window we install. Two panes with an argon or krypton gas fill significantly reduce heat transfer. Low-E (low-emissivity) coatings on the glass surface reflect infrared heat in winter and block solar gain in summer — important on south- and west-facing windows where afternoon sun is most intense. <a href="https://www.energystar.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ENERGY STAR</a>-certified double-pane windows carry the Northern Zone rating appropriate for Pennsylvania's climate. Look for the <a href="https://www.nfrc.org/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">NFRC</a> label to compare U-factor and Solar Heat Gain Coefficient (SHGC) across brands.</p>

              <h3>Triple-Pane Windows</h3>
              <p>Triple-pane adds a third layer of insulating gas, lowering the U-factor further. We install them on very exposed walls — large north-facing openings in drafty older homes, or in finished basements where grade-level cold transfer is an issue. For most interior-facing or partially-shielded openings, the incremental energy savings over high-quality double-pane do not offset the cost premium (typically 25–40% more per unit). We will tell you honestly if triple-pane is justified in your situation.</p>

              <h3>Gas Fills and Spacers</h3>
              <p>The gas between panes (argon is standard, krypton is used in thinner spacers for triple-pane applications) matters more than most homeowners realize. So does the spacer — a warm-edge spacer reduces condensation at the glass edge, which is where most heat loss occurs at the perimeter. We specify warm-edge spacers on all energy-efficiency-focused installs.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section post-body__section--new reveal" id="from-workshop">
              <h2>From the Workshop: What Daniil Notices</h2>

              <div class="post-workshop">
                <div class="post-workshop__header">
                  <div class="post-workshop__avatar" aria-hidden="true">DA</div>
                  <div>
                    <p class="post-workshop__name">Daniil Akulov</p>
