                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="post-workshop__body">
                  <h3 style="color:#fff;margin:0 0 1rem;">Non-Standard Rough Openings in Pre-1970 Delaware County Homes</h3>
                  <p>In older Delaware County homes — particularly the pre-1970 stock in Media, Springfield, and Nether Providence — we almost always find non-standard rough openings when we open up the wall. Standard replacement windows are sized for modern framing dimensions. Older homes were often framed to different specifications, and decades of settling, painting over sills, and previous patch repairs add further variation.</p>
                  <p>Our assessment process before ordering any windows:</p>
                  <ol class="post-workshop__list">
                    <li><strong>Measure the opening in three places</strong> — top, middle, and bottom. They are rarely identical in an older home. The smallest dimension governs the order size.</li>
                    <li><strong>Check the head and sill for rot or soft wood.</strong> A water-damaged sill discovered after the window arrives causes delays and additional cost. We probe every opening at the estimate stage.</li>
                    <li><strong>Assess the rough-opening depth.</strong> Older exterior walls were often built with different stud depths than today's standard 2x4 or 2x6 construction. The window jamb depth has to match, or extensions are required.</li>
                    <li><strong>Confirm the header.</strong> Bay windows and any opening enlargement require a properly sized structural header. We verify load path before recommending any window that changes the opening size.</li>
                    <li><strong>Account for interior and exterior trim.</strong> Removing old trim often reveals surprises — plaster "keyed" into the original window frame, or lead paint that requires containment procedures under Pennsylvania DEP rules.</li>
                  </ol>
                  <p>We order windows only after these measurements are confirmed on-site. It adds one extra visit to the project timeline, but it prevents the far more costly problem of a window that doesn't fit.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section post-body__section--new reveal" id="permit-requirements">
              <h2>PA UCC Permit Requirements — When Does Window Replacement Need a Permit?</h2>
              <p>Pennsylvania's Uniform Construction Code (PA UCC) governs residential building work statewide, with local enforcement handled by individual townships. For window work in Delaware County, here is how the permit requirement typically breaks down:</p>
              <ul class="post-list">
                <li><strong>Like-for-like replacement (same opening, same size):</strong> Permits generally not required in most Delaware County townships. This is the most common scenario — removing an old window and installing a new one in the same rough opening.</li>
                <li><strong>Enlarging or relocating an opening:</strong> Requires a permit. Any structural change to the opening — widening, lowering the sill, moving it to a different location — involves altering a load-bearing element of the exterior wall.</li>
                <li><strong>New egress window in a bedroom or finished basement:</strong> Permits are required, and the window must meet IRC Section R310 egress specifications (minimum net clear opening area, height, width, and sill height). Delaware County building departments enforce these specifications for code compliance and for insurance purposes.</li>
                <li><strong>Historic overlay districts:</strong> Some neighborhoods in Media Borough and other municipalities have historic overlay zoning. Window replacements in these areas may require a Certificate of Appropriateness from the local Historic Architectural Review Board (HARB) before permits are issued. We work in several of these districts and are familiar with the approval process.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>Permit costs in Delaware County typically range from <strong>$100–$400</strong> for residential window permits depending on the municipality and scope of work. Processing times range from 1–3 weeks for straightforward applications. ProBrothers handles permit applications for all work we perform — this is included as part of our service, not an add-on.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section post-body__section--new reveal" id="cost-data">
              <h2>What Does Window Replacement Cost in Delaware County?</h2>
              <p>Cost ranges for Delaware County and the greater Philadelphia metro area, based on our project work and the <a href="https://www.remodeling.hw.net/cost-vs-value/2025/philadelphia-pa/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Remodeling Magazine Cost vs. Value Report (Philadelphia metro)</a>:</p>

