                    </tr>
                  </tfoot>
                </table>
              </div>
              <p>The Remodeling Magazine data for the Philadelphia metro area shows mid-range window replacement recovering approximately <strong>65–72% of cost at resale</strong>. That said, the comfort and energy cost reduction of replacing drafty single-pane originals in an older home often makes the financial argument before resale is ever a factor.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section post-body__section--new reveal" id="case-studies">
              <h2>ProBrothers Window Projects in Delaware County</h2>
              <p>Two examples from our project work that illustrate how window selection actually plays out in real homes:</p>

              <div class="post-case-study">
                <p class="post-case-study__label">Project Case Study #1</p>
                <h4 class="post-case-study__title">BlackHorse House &mdash; Full Home Remodel, Media PA</h4>
                <p>A full home remodel in Media, Pennsylvania that included structural reconfiguration of the main entry and window placement. The original side entrance was relocated to the center of the facade, with new windows added symmetrically on either side. This is a case where window style was driven entirely by architectural intent, not just replacement — the new windows had to match the proportions of the restored facade and complement the entry door surround. We used double-hung vinyl windows with divided lite grilles to preserve the home's original character while meeting current energy efficiency standards. The rough-opening work required header reinforcement because the original framing had been built around the prior (off-center) door placement.</p>
                <p>This project is one of 135+ ProBrothers has completed across Delaware County and the Main Line.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="post-case-study">
                <p class="post-case-study__label">Project Case Study #2</p>
                <h4 class="post-case-study__title">Window Casing Project &mdash; Delaware County</h4>
                <p>A window casing and trim installation project focused on the finish work around window openings — a detail that has a significant impact on how new windows integrate with the surrounding interior. Window casing in older Delaware County homes often involves matching existing profile millwork, which requires custom milling or sourcing period-appropriate trim stock. Getting the casing right is as important as the window unit itself for the finished appearance.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Ventilation and Climate Considerations</h2>
              <p>Delaware County sits in Pennsylvania's mixed-humid climate zone — hot, humid summers and cold winters with significant freeze-thaw cycling. Window selection needs to account for both ends of the thermal spectrum.</p>

              <h3>Summer Ventilation</h3>
              <p>Casement and awning windows create a "scoop" effect, directing prevailing breezes into a room more efficiently than double-hung windows. In homes without central air, this can significantly improve comfort in upstairs rooms where heat accumulates. We often recommend casement windows on the side of a home that faces the prevailing southwest summer wind.</p>

              <h3>Winter Air Sealing</h3>
              <p>The most common source of winter heat loss in older Delaware County homes is not poor glass performance — it is air infiltration at the window perimeter. New windows installed with proper foam backing, backing rod, and exterior caulking can reduce drafts far more than a glass upgrade alone. Our installation process includes a full air-seal of the perimeter on every window we set.</p>

              <h3>Freeze-Thaw and Frame Performance</h3>
              <p>Pennsylvania's freeze-thaw cycling puts stress on window frames, especially where dissimilar materials meet (wood sill, vinyl frame, exterior casing). Fiberglass frames handle thermal cycling best; premium vinyl performs well. Wood frames require more maintenance to keep the seals intact over time.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Aesthetic Appeal and Home Value</h2>
              <p>Windows are one of the most visible elements of a home's exterior, and they have an outsized effect on curb appeal relative to their cost. A few things we pay close attention to:</p>
              <ul class="post-list">
                <li><strong>Proportion:</strong> Windows that are the wrong size for their opening — either too small or too large — look off even if the style is correct. We specify window dimensions based on the room's scale and the opening's relationship to adjacent architectural elements.</li>
                <li><strong>Grille pattern:</strong> In colonial and craftsman homes throughout Delaware County, divided lite grilles (either true divided or simulated) maintain the historical rhythm of the facade. In contemporary additions, clean-line windows without grilles typically read better.</li>
                <li><strong>Exterior color and finish:</strong> Window frame color should coordinate with the exterior trim scheme. We match or complement existing trim colors rather than defaulting to a stock white, particularly on homes with a strong architectural identity.</li>
                <li><strong>Interior integration:</strong> New windows installed without matching interior casing look unfinished. We include casing work in our window project scoping by default — it is the only way the installation looks complete from inside.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>On resale, updated windows are a visible signal of maintenance investment. Real estate agents consistently flag original single-pane or failed-seal double-pane windows as a negotiating point; new replacement windows remove that objection and frequently show up as a justification for asking price.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Window Replacement</h2>
              <div class="post-faq">
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>What window styles does ProBrothers most commonly install in Delaware County homes?</h3>
                  <p>We most frequently install double-hung windows (ideal for Delaware County's colonial and craftsman housing stock), casement windows for side-facing or hard-to-reach openings, and picture windows in living rooms where ventilation is secondary to light. The right style depends on your home's original window count, rough-opening dimensions, and how each room is used.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Does window replacement in Delaware County require a permit?</h3>
                  <p>Like-for-like replacement in the same opening usually does not require a permit in most Delaware County townships. However, you will need one if you are enlarging or relocating an opening, creating a new egress window in a bedroom or finished basement, or working on a structure in an historic overlay district. ProBrothers assesses permit requirements as part of every estimate — at no additional charge.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>What does window installation cost in Delaware County, PA?</h3>
                  <p>Budget installations (vinyl, basic Low-E double-pane) typically run $400–$700 per unit installed. Mid-range (premium vinyl or fiberglass, custom sizes) runs $700–$1,200 per unit. Premium (wood-clad, architectural grade, bay/bow configurations) starts at $1,200 per unit. Homes with non-standard rough openings — common in pre-1970 Delaware County homes — may require additional framing work. <a href="/contact/">Request a free estimate</a> for your specific project.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>What is the difference between double-pane and triple-pane windows for Pennsylvania's climate?</h3>
                  <p>Double-pane windows with Low-E coating handle most Delaware County homes well — they significantly reduce heat loss in winter and solar gain in summer. Triple-pane windows add a third layer of insulating gas, which helps in very exposed locations (large north-facing walls, unshielded second stories). For most interior or partially-shielded openings, the cost premium of triple-pane does not produce proportional energy savings. We make this recommendation based on each opening's orientation and your home's overall energy profile.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How do I choose the right window materials for my home?</h3>
                  <p>Vinyl frames are low-maintenance and cost-effective — a reliable choice for most Delaware County homes. Fiberglass offers superior dimensional stability and handles Pennsylvania's temperature swings well. Wood-clad windows preserve the original character of pre-1970 homes and are often required in historic overlay districts. We match the material recommendation to your home's age, architectural style, and long-term maintenance preference.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How long does window installation take?</h3>
                  <p>Most standard window installations take 1–3 days depending on the number of units and complexity. Homes with non-standard rough openings (common in Delaware County homes built before 1970) may require additional time for framing adjustments. We provide a clear timeline at the estimate stage so there are no surprises.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
